GETPWENT(3)                    P32W Unix                     GETPWENT(3)

NAME
       getpwent, getpwnam, getpwuid, setpwent, endpwent - passwd entries

SYNOPSIS
       #include <pwd.h>

       struct passwd *getpwent(void);
       struct passwd *getpwnam(const char *name);
       struct passwd *getpwuid(int uid);
       void setpwent(void);
       void endpwent(void);

       struct passwd {
           char *pw_name;      /* login name */
           char *pw_passwd;    /* hashed password, "" or "*" */
           int pw_uid, pw_gid; /* user and group ids */
           char *pw_gecos;     /* full name, comment */
           char *pw_dir;       /* home directory */
           char *pw_shell;     /* login shell */
       };

DESCRIPTION
       These read the password file /etc/passwd, whose lines are

           name:password:uid:gid:full name:home:shell

       (passwd(5)). getpwent opens the file on its first call and
       returns the next entry each time; lines starting with # and
       empty lines are skipped. An empty shell field reads as
       /bin/sh. setpwent goes back to the first entry; endpwent
       closes the file.

       getpwnam returns the first entry with login name name, and
       getpwuid the first with user id uid. Each reads the file from
       the start and closes it afterwards.

       pw_passwd is empty for no password, * for an account that
       cannot log in, or the hash made by pwhash(3), which pwcheck
       compares with a password typed.

RETURN VALUE
       A pointer to the entry, or NULL at the end of the file, when
       no entry matches, or when /etc/passwd cannot be opened. The
       entry and its strings are static, overwritten by the next
       call of any of these functions.

EXAMPLES
       struct passwd *pw = getpwuid(getuid());
       printf("%s\n", pw ? pw->pw_name : "?");

       while ((pw = getpwent()) != NULL)
           printf("%-8s %s\n", pw->pw_name, pw->pw_dir);
       endpwent();

FILES
       /etc/passwd

SEE ALSO
       passwd(1), id(1), pwhash(3), getgrent(3), passwd(5)

BUGS
       getpwnam and getpwuid close the file, so a getpwent loop that
       calls them starts again from the top. A line longer than 255
       characters is cut into pieces that read as separate entries.
       There is no shadow file and no getpwnam_r. The numbers are
       read with atoi(3), without checking.
